{
  "_note": "Tree height (m) from DBH (cm), forms follow the Urban Tree Database TS4 table: lin a+bx, quad +cx^2, cub +dx^3, loglogwN exp(a+b*ln(ln(x+1))+w*c/2), expowN exp(a+bx+w*c/2) with w = 1, sqrt x, x, x^2 for N = 1..4, plus pow a*x^b. DBH is clamped to [dbh_min, dbh_max], the range the species was measured over; lookup order: full botanical name, genus + species, genus (a proxy species noted in `proxy`); sources: Peper et al. 2014, Oakville ON ash study (src oakville) and McPherson, van Doorn, Peper 2016 Urban Tree Database (src utd:<region>). UTD regions in priority order: MidWst Minneapolis, LoMidW Indianapolis, NoEast Queens, NMtnPr Fort Collins, PacfNW Longview, TpIntW Boise, InterW Albuquerque; four species with no fit in those regions (tulip tree, black locust, eastern red cedar, flowering dogwood) use the largest warmer-region sample for the same species and are flagged with `note`. Tree of heaven (src qingdao+fia) is a power law through the Qingdao street-tree sample mean, 27.4 cm and 11.7 m (Forests 2023, 14(3):472, n = 73; the paper publishes only a 95% quantile fit, in log10 despite its ln notation), with slope 0.321 ± 0.122 pooled by inverse variance from the Qingdao fit (0.328 ± 0.265) and a log-log fit to USFS FIA open-grown and dominant trees in MD, PA, NJ, DE, WV, OH, NY (0.319 ± 0.138, n = 21; all crown classes give 0.50, steepened by forest competition); dbh_max extends to the FIA maximum. Genera with no urban equation anywhere (lilac, serviceberry, Thuja cedar, rowan, katsura, fir, ironwood, hazel, yellowwood, willow, yew, cork tree, hemlock) get no estimate",
